#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Purpose: Squash-aware local branch prune (DS-153). Deletes only local
         branches whose LOCAL TIP content is PROVEN to be on the base
         branch, via a four-layer, first-match-wins subsumption predicate
         (ancestry, squash-patch equivalence, tip-subsumption, content-on-
         main), rather than the forbidden "a PR merged" predicate. Absence
         of proof is always a skip, never a delete - the failure mode this
         ticket forbids (deleting sole-copy work) is structurally
         unreachable by the predicate itself; see the module docstring of
         bin/tests/worktree_model.py and `.agentic/ds-153-plan.md`
         (DS-153) for the full rationale and the Skeptic amendments (B1-B14)
         this implementation follows.

Public API (CLI):
  ds-branch-prune [--repo <path>] [--base <ref>] [--explain] [--dry-run]
                  [--pr-data <file>] [--no-gh] [--nudge-threshold <int>]

  --repo <path>            Repository to operate on. Default: cwd.
  --base <ref>             Base ref to prove subsumption against. Default:
                            NONE - resolved automatically via
                            `resolve_base_branch` (bin/_lib.py), the same
                            shared resolver bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees uses:
                            an AGENTS.md `BASE_BRANCH:` declaration
                            (AUTHORITATIVE - declared-but-unresolvable
                            fails outright, never falls through), then
                            `git symbolic-ref refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`,
                            then a local `develop`, then a local
                            `development`, then `main` falling back to
                            `master`. An explicit --base is still used
                            VERBATIM with no validation. A local guard set
                            of {"main", "master", "develop", "development",
                            <local name of the RESOLVED base>} is never
                            eligible for deletion (guard G0) -
                            develop/development are included
                            unconditionally, independent of what resolution
                            picked, so a develop-based workflow's
                            integration branch is protected on every run.
                            This default was "origin/main" until the
                            worktree-reap calibration work. That hardcoding
                            meant that on any repo whose integration branch
                            is not `main` - the exact case the resolver
                            exists for - every branch was evaluated against
                            the wrong base and the run proved nothing. The
                            n3 disclosure that used to sit here (a
                            non-develop declared BASE_BRANCH being deleted
                            via L1 because G0 could not see it) is
                            CLOSED by that resolution: `base_local` in
                            main() is now the actually-resolved base.
                            Residual hazard, disclosed not hidden: with
                            `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` unset locally, the
                            tier order falls through to the local-`develop`
                            tier, so a `main`-based repo that happens to
                            carry an unrelated local `develop` branch
                            silently resolves to `origin/develop`. This
                            risk already existed for
                            bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees; extending the
                            resolver here extends it to branch DELETION,
                            which is more consequential. It is bounded, not
                            closed: `develop` is unconditionally guarded by
                            G0, the resolved base is printed in the summary
                            line on every run, and an explicit --base
                            overrides. Set origin/HEAD
                            (`git remote set-head origin -a`) or declare
                            BASE_BRANCH in AGENTS.md to make resolution
                            deterministic.
  --explain                Print one line per branch with its outcome
                            (default: aggregate counts only).
  --dry-run                Compute and report; delete nothing, write no
                            ledger entries.
  --pr-data <file>         Read merged-PR JSON from this file instead of
                            invoking `gh` (test-injection seam; also usable
                            for caching). A missing or malformed file is a
                            usage error (exit 1) - never silently degrades.
  --no-gh                  Force degraded mode (empty candidate set; only
                            L1/L4 evidence can fire) without attempting to
                            invoke `gh` at all.
  --nudge-threshold <int>  SKIP_UNPROVEN count at/above which a one-line
                            "accumulating" hint is printed. Default: 20.

The predicate (guards, then layers, first match wins - see
`.agentic/ds-153-plan.md` "The predicate (normative)" for the full
normative table):
  G0 SKIP_BASE_BRANCH      - branch is main/master/--base's local name
  G1 SKIP_CHECKED_OUT      - branch is checked out in any linked worktree
  G2 SKIP_NO_MERGE_BASE    - no merge-base against --base
  L1 ancestor               - `merge-base --is-ancestor B M` (DELETE)
  L2 squash-patch-equiv     - patch-id(diff(base,O)) == patch-id(diff(MC^,MC))
                              for a candidate merged PR's mergeCommit MC on
                              M, PLUS a byte-for-byte hunk-content
                              discrimination check (Amendment B2 - closes
                              git patch-id's whitespace-insensitivity hole)
                              (DELETE)
  L3 tip-subsumed            - candidate PR's mergeCommit MC on M, head H
                              local, and O is an ancestor of H (DELETE)
  L4 content-on-main         - files B changed (base..O) are a non-empty
                              set disjoint from files changed M..O
                              (two-dot, per Amendment B8) (DELETE)
  otherwise SKIP_UNPROVEN

Every branch's guard/layer decision is additionally resolved through
`disposition_for_orphan_branch` (bin/tests/worktree_model.py) so this
script and every other branch-deletion caller share one normative
ELIGIBLE/SKIP_PR_MERGED_UNPROVEN definition (DS-153 Amendment B1) rather
than two representations that can silently disagree - see that module's
docstring. `SKIP_PR_MERGED_UNPROVEN` and the model's final-fallback
`SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR` both display here as `SKIP_UNPROVEN`, matching the
plan's own outcome vocabulary; a bare `pr_state == "MERGED"` alone is
never sufficient for deletion (this is the model's TERMINAL skip - see
worktree_model.py Amendment B1).

Deletion mechanism: `git branch -D` (not `-d` - `-d`'s own ancestry safety
check is exactly the signal proven inert on squash-merged branches here;
`-D` is safe at this call site ONLY because the predicate above has
already established subsumption). Amendment B3: `git branch -D` deletes
the branch's own reflog outright (`.git/logs/refs/heads/<b>`), so the
recovery path is NOT the 90-day reflog default - every successful deletion
is recorded as `<branch> <tip-sha>` to BOTH stdout and an append-only
ledger file (`.agentic/branch-prune-ledger.txt` under --repo), so recovery
is always `git branch <name> <sha>` regardless of reflog state. Amendment
B11: a branch whose `-D` fails (e.g. git refuses a branch checked out or
being rebased in another worktree it did not already know about) is
reported to stderr and the run continues - never a hard failure.

Upstream deps: Python 3 stdlib only (argparse, json, subprocess, shutil,
               sys, pathlib, collections). `git` (CLI) and, unless
               --no-gh/--pr-data, `gh` (CLI, for the bulk merged-PR list).
               bin/tests/worktree_model.py (DispositionFacts, Disposition,
               DEFAULT_BASE_BRANCHES, disposition_for_orphan_branch,
               parse_porcelain - imported by path, not installed).
               bin/_lib.py (resolve_base_branch - likewise imported by
               resolved path, via a `sys.path` insert of this file's own
               bin/ directory). `_lib` also exports a `_run`; this file
               keeps its own same-named, different-signature `_run` and
               does not import or unify with that one.

Downstream consumers: content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md (§Session-
                      start prune script and §Branch prune, DS-153 unit 2 -
                      the PATH-guarded `if command -v ds-branch-prune`
                      invocation, Amendment B9), content/commands/ds-cleanup-
                      worktrees.md Step 5 (the same invocation, referenced by
                      pointer), and docs/slides/worktree-lifecycle-slides.md
                      (the built-deck copy of the session-start prune block).
                      CI: bin/tests/test_branch_prune.py (pytest, auto-
                      collected by `python3 -m pytest bin/tests/`).
                      Shares bin/_lib.py's resolve_base_branch with
                      bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees, so a change to the tier
                      order affects BOTH tools - see that module's own
                      manifest.
                      `bin/agentic-branch-prune` is a plain symlink to this
                      file for naming consistency with the established
                      `bin/ds-base-sync` primary-name-plus-symlink pattern
                      (Amendment B14: NOT back-compat - no prior name for
                      this tool existed before DS-153).

Failure modes: exit 0 on success INCLUDING partial degradation (gh
               unavailable/erroring/--no-gh, individual `git branch -D`
               failures) and INCLUDING an unresolvable base branch - this
               is designed to run at session start and must never look
               like a failure merely because branches were skipped or one
               delete was refused elsewhere. An unresolvable base fails
               SAFE on the destructive side: every candidate tried is
               named on stderr, the summary line reads
               `base=unresolved mode=<composed> branches=0 deletions=0
               skips=0` - `<composed>` is `_compose_mode`'s output with
               `skipped=True`, so it is a bare `skipped` on a plain run and
               `skipped, dry-run` (and/or `, degraded (gh unavailable)`)
               when those axes also hold, never a hardcoded single word
               that would make a --dry-run run indistinguishable from a
               live one - NOTHING is deleted, NO ledger entry is written,
               and the
               exit code is 0. Exit 1 ONLY on an internal/usage error:
               --repo is not a directory or not a git repository,
               --pr-data is missing or not valid JSON (validated BEFORE
               base resolution, deliberately, so a usage error is never
               downgraded to the exit-0 skip above), or an unhandled
               exception during evidence computation. The merged-PR
               window is fetched by cursor-paginated GraphQL and its
               completeness is PROVEN, not assumed: the number of nodes
               retrieved must equal the `pullRequests` connection's own
               `totalCount`. Any other outcome - a count mismatch, an
               absent totalCount, a mid-pagination API/parse failure, a
               GraphQL `errors` array, or exhausting the PR_MAX_PAGES
               safety bound with pages still pending - is reported as an
               INCOMPLETE window: a one-line NOTE naming the reason
               (Amendment B13's contract, retained) and the same guarantee
               that absence of a match is never proof an unmatched branch
               is unmerged. An incomplete or entirely absent candidate set
               can only cause MORE skips, never a false delete. Extra or
               wrong rows are inert too, but for a narrower reason than
               "no row is ever trusted": every layer re-checks the row
               against LOCAL objects before acting on it (L2 re-derives
               patch-id and byte-level hunk content; L3 requires the
               mergeCommit to be an ancestor of the base ref and the
               branch tip to be an ancestor of the recorded head). L3 does
               take one thing from the row on trust - the PAIRING of
               `headRefOid` with `mergeCommit`, which it never verifies by
               checking that the head is contained in the merge commit.
               That is pre-existing and sound for genuine API rows, and it
               is why this manifest claims a re-check, not an absence of
               trust. This NOTE previously fired on EVERY run against this
               repository, because the superseded 500-row `gh pr list`
               ceiling sat below its merged-PR count; it is now expected to
               be silent.

Performance: one `gh repo view` slug lookup plus one `gh api graphql`
             call per 100 merged PRs (or a single file read via
             --pr-data, which makes zero network calls), plus a handful of
             `git` invocations per local branch
             (merge-base, is-ancestor, diff, patch-id). Every `gh` call is
             bounded by GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS and the pagination loop as
             a whole by PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS, so worst-case latency is
             bounded rather than a multiple of the page count.

             Wall-clock, MEASURED (not estimated) on this repository's own
             checkout on 2026-09-07 - 175 local branches, 770 merged PRs,
             `--repo . --dry-run`, median of three runs on a warm cache:
             10.1s before the switch to paginated GraphQL and 14.0s after.
             The increase is the additional network round trips: the
             superseded fetch made ONE capped `gh pr list` call, this one
             made nine (a slug lookup plus eight pages) on that same
             measured run. State the branch count,
             the merged-PR count and the date alongside any figure quoted
             from here - all three inputs grow monotonically, so a bare
             number goes stale silently. (A prior revision of this
             paragraph claimed "sub-second on this repo's ~49-branch
             checkout"; that was already false by roughly an order of
             magnitude before the change that prompted this correction.)
             Not designed for repositories with very large branch counts
             (see the plan's Known limitations for a deferred
             `.agentic/`-cached-PR-JSON follow-up).
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import json
import shutil
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from collections import Counter
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Set, Tuple

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared helpers: worktree_model.py (bin/tests/), imported by resolved path
# so a bare PATH-symlink invocation
# (~/.local/bin/ds-branch-prune -> repo bin/ds-branch-prune) still finds
# it - .resolve() follows the symlink back to the real bin/ directory,
# matching the pattern established in bin/ds-feedback.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
_BIN_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sys.path.insert(0, str(_BIN_DIR / "tests"))
# bin/ itself, for `_lib`, which owns the ONE base-branch resolver this
# script and bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees share. Same resolved-path rationale
# as the line above: a bare PATH-symlink invocation must still find it.
sys.path.insert(0, str(_BIN_DIR))

from worktree_model import (  # noqa: E402
    DEFAULT_BASE_BRANCHES,
    Disposition,
    DispositionFacts,
    disposition_for_orphan_branch,
    parse_porcelain,
)

# Only `resolve_base_branch` is imported. `_lib` also exports a `_run`, but
# this file keeps its OWN same-named, different-signature `_run` below
# (`input_text`, which `_lib`'s copy does not accept) that every call site
# here depends on -
# the two are deliberately NOT unified, and the shared one is deliberately
# NOT imported here even under an alias, because nothing in this file would
# call it (`resolve_base_branch` uses `_lib`'s copy internally, from inside
# `_lib`, needing no import on this side). An unused alias would be dead
# code that reads like a live dependency.
from _lib import resolve_base_branch  # noqa: E402


def _compose_mode(degraded: bool, dry_run: bool, skipped: bool = False) -> str:
    """Compose the summary line's `mode=` field from EVERY axis that
    changed this run's behavior, never from whichever one won a tiebreak.

    `skipped` (base unresolvable), `degraded` (gh unavailable) and
    `dry_run` are independent and can hold in any combination; each
    contributes its own comma-separated part, and "live" is emitted ONLY
    when no axis is set. This function is the single composer for both
    summary lines in this file specifically so the unresolvable-base line
    cannot drift back into hardcoding one axis: an earlier revision of
    this tool computed `mode = "degraded" if degraded else "live"` and
    never consulted `args.dry_run`, so a --dry-run run honored the flag
    while printing the single most dangerous possible misreport for a
    branch-deletion tool, `mode=live`. The unresolvable-base line then
    reintroduced the same shape by hardcoding `mode=skipped`.
    """
    parts = []
    if skipped:
        parts.append("skipped")
    if degraded:
        parts.append("degraded (gh unavailable)")
    if dry_run:
        parts.append("dry-run")
    return ", ".join(parts) if parts else "live"


# Merged-PR fetch bounds. A `PR_LIST_LIMIT = 500` constant (a
# `gh pr list --limit` ceiling) used to live here and is deliberately gone
# rather than merely raised: it capped the merged-PR window at 500 rows,
# which this repository's own history had already outgrown (770 merged PRs
# at the time of this change), so the truncation flag was set on literally
# every run and L2/L3 evidence was degraded permanently. Because the cap
# was bounded by merged-PR count, which only ever grows, no amount of
# branch cleanup could relieve it. The fetch below is now a
# cursor-paginated GraphQL query whose completeness is PROVEN against the
# connection's own `totalCount` rather than assumed from a row count.
#
# PR_PAGE_SIZE - nodes requested per GraphQL page (100 is GitHub's maximum
#                page size for this connection).
# PR_MAX_PAGES - a genuine safety bound on the pagination loop, NOT a
#                result cap in the old sense. Exhausting it while the
#                connection still reports `hasNextPage` is treated as
#                INCOMPLETE evidence (NOTE printed, absence of a match
#                still never proof of unmerged), never as a complete
#                window. It bounds worst-case work at
#                PR_PAGE_SIZE * PR_MAX_PAGES = 10,000 merged PRs.
PR_PAGE_SIZE = 100
PR_MAX_PAGES = 100

# Network bounds for the merged-PR fetch. This tool runs SYNCHRONOUSLY at
# session start (see content/references/worktree-lifecycle.md), so a hung
# `gh` blocks the operator, and pagination raised the worst-case number of
# network invocations from 1 to PR_MAX_PAGES + 1. Two bounds, because a
# per-call timeout alone would still admit a ~101x multiple of itself:
#
# GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS - per `gh` invocation, passed to subprocess.
# PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS - wall-clock ceiling on the whole pagination
#                             loop, checked before each request is issued.
#
# Worst case is therefore PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS plus one in-flight
# call, not PR_MAX_PAGES * GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS. BOTH bounds resolve to
# an INCOMPLETE window and never to a complete one, and both failure
# directions are safe: a timeout that fires too eagerly loses rows, which
# can only cost deletions, and a timeout that never fires leaves behavior
# exactly as it is without them.
GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 30.0
PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS = 120.0
LEDGER_RELATIVE_PATH = ".agentic/branch-prune-ledger.txt"

# Amendment B2: every diff invocation is pinned to a config-independent
# option set. `diff.algorithm=myers` and `diff.noprefix=false` neutralize a
# caller's ~/.gitconfig; `--no-ext-diff` neutralizes diff.external;
# `--no-renames` is determinism per the plan's original spec.
_DIFF_CONFIG_PIN = ["-c", "diff.algorithm=myers", "-c", "diff.noprefix=false"]


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# git plumbing (thin wrappers; every call pins options per Amendment B2)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _run(
    args: List[str],
    cwd: Optional[str] = None,
    input_text: Optional[str] = None,
    timeout: Optional[float] = None,
) -> subprocess.CompletedProcess:
    """`timeout=None` (the default) is the pre-existing unbounded behavior
    that every local-plumbing call site here relies on; those are fast and
    offline, and bounding them would add a failure mode for no gain. A
    non-None `timeout` lets `subprocess.TimeoutExpired` propagate to the
    caller, which every `gh` call site below catches and converts into
    degraded-or-incomplete evidence - never into a delete.
    """
    return subprocess.run(
        args, cwd=cwd, input=input_text, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=timeout
    )


def _resolve_repo(path: str) -> str:
    p = Path(path).expanduser()
    if not p.is_dir():
        raise ValueError(f"not a directory: {path}")
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", str(p), "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise ValueError(f"not a git repository: {path}")
    return proc.stdout.strip()


def _list_local_branches(repo: str) -> List[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(refname:short)", "refs/heads/"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"git for-each-ref failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}")
    return [line for line in proc.stdout.splitlines() if line]


def _checked_out_branches(repo: str) -> Set[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain"])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        raise RuntimeError(f"git worktree list failed: {proc.stderr.strip()}")
    entries = parse_porcelain(proc.stdout)
    return {e.branch for e in entries if e.branch}


def _rev_parse(repo: str, ref: str) -> Optional[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "rev-parse", ref])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return proc.stdout.strip()


def _merge_base(repo: str, a: str, b: str) -> Optional[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "merge-base", a, b])
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    return proc.stdout.strip()


def _is_ancestor(repo: str, ancestor: str, descendant: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", ancestor, descendant])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _commit_exists(repo: str, sha: str) -> bool:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "cat-file", "-e", f"{sha}^{{commit}}"])
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _upstream(repo: str, branch: str) -> Optional[str]:
    proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "for-each-ref", "--format=%(upstream:short)", f"refs/heads/{branch}"])
    val = proc.stdout.strip()
    return val or None


def _diff(repo: str, rev_a: str, rev_b: str) -> str:
    # Amendment B8: two-dot explicitly (`rev_a..rev_b`) - never three-dot,
    # which would silently make L4 inert (own ∩ cur == own, never empty).
    proc = _run(
        ["git", "-C", repo, *_DIFF_CONFIG_PIN, "diff", "--no-ext-diff", "-U3", "--no-renames", f"{rev_a}..{rev_b}"]
    )
    return proc.stdout


def _diff_names(repo: str, rev_a: str, rev_b: str) -> Set[str]:
    # Amendment B2 item 1 (m3 fix): pinned identically to _diff() above -
    # L4 is the sole surviving degraded-mode layer and its own file-name
    # comparison must not depend on the invoking user's ~/.gitconfig either.
    proc = _run(
        [
            "git",
            "-C",
            repo,
            *_DIFF_CONFIG_PIN,
            "diff",
            "--no-ext-diff",
            "--name-only",
            "--no-renames",
            f"{rev_a}..{rev_b}",
        ]
    )
    return {line for line in proc.stdout.splitlines() if line}


def _patch_id(diff_text: str) -> Optional[str]:
    if not diff_text.strip():
        return None
    # Amendment B2 item 2: --stable specified explicitly, never the default.
    proc = _run(["git", "patch-id", "--stable"], input_text=diff_text)
    line = proc.stdout.strip()
    if not line:
        return None
    return line.split()[0]


def _content_signature(diff_text: str) -> str:
    """Amendment B2 item 3: the whitespace-hole discrimination check.

    `git patch-id` strips whitespace before hashing - two diffs differing
    only by whitespace (e.g. "X  Y" vs "X Y") produce the IDENTICAL patch
    id. This function strips ONLY the line-number portion of each `@@`
    hunk header (preserving L2's non-decay property, which comes from
    line-number insensitivity) and leaves every hunk CONTENT line -
    including whitespace - untouched, so two diffs that patch-id-match but
    differ in actual content (including whitespace-only differences)
    produce different signatures here.

    m1 fix (Skeptic round 2): also drops `diff --git a/... b/...`,
    `index <old>..<new> ...`, `--- a/...`, and `+++ b/...` lines. Those
    lines are NOT hunk content - `index` in particular encodes the blob
    hashes of the file's PRE- and POST-image, which are a function of the
    surrounding file's full text at each comparison point, not of the
    delta itself. Two diffs applying the IDENTICAL logical change at
    different surrounding-file states (e.g. one repo state has three lines
    prepended relative to the other) are legitimately non-decaying per L2's
    own proof, but previously produced different signatures here solely
    because their `index` blob hashes differed - a false SKIP_UNPROVEN that
    silently defeated L2's stated non-decay property (reproduced: a branch
    changing `line5` off an older `main`, whose identical change is squashed
    onto a `main` that has since gained 3 prepended lines, patch-id-matches
    at `line8` but previously failed this discrimination check). Excluding
    these path/hash lines restores non-decay while still rejecting a
    genuine whitespace-only divergence, because whitespace differences live
    in the hunk content lines this function keeps untouched.

    n1 fix (Skeptic round 3): the header-line filter is POSITIONAL, not a
    blanket prefix match. `line.startswith("--- ")`/`"+++ "` applied to
    every line also swallows hunk CONTENT lines that happen to start with
    the same three characters - e.g. a deleted line whose text begins
    `-- ` renders as `--- <text>` in the raw diff (the diff's own leading
    `-` plus the two-dash content), and an added line beginning `++ `
    renders as `+++ <text>`. Both would previously be dropped from the
    signature, silently reopening the whitespace/content hole this
    function exists to close for that line class. Each header line
    (`index `, `--- `, `+++ `) is now consumed at most ONCE per file
    block, immediately following the `diff --git` line that opens it, and
    the consume-flags are cleared the moment a `@@` hunk header is seen -
    after which no further line in that file's diff can be mistaken for a
    header line.
    """
    out = []
    consume_index = consume_minus = consume_plus = False
    for line in diff_text.splitlines():
        if line.startswith("diff --git "):
            consume_index = consume_minus = consume_plus = True
            continue
        if line.startswith("@@"):
            consume_index = consume_minus = consume_plus = False
            idx = line.find("@@", 2)
            out.append("@@" + line[idx + 2 :] if idx != -1 else "@@")
            continue
        if consume_index and line.startswith("index "):
            consume_index = False
            continue
        if consume_minus and line.startswith("--- "):
            consume_minus = False
            continue
        if consume_plus and line.startswith("+++ "):
            consume_plus = False
            continue
        out.append(line)
    return "\n".join(out)


def _l2_match(repo: str, base_sha: str, tip_sha: str, mc: str) -> bool:
    diff_branch = _diff(repo, base_sha, tip_sha)
    diff_mc = _diff(repo, f"{mc}^", mc)
    pid_branch = _patch_id(diff_branch)
    pid_mc = _patch_id(diff_mc)
    if pid_branch is None or pid_mc is None:
        return False
    if pid_branch != pid_mc:
        return False
    return _content_signature(diff_branch) == _content_signature(diff_mc)


def _l4_match(repo: str, base_sha: str, tip_sha: str, base_ref: str) -> bool:
    own = _diff_names(repo, base_sha, tip_sha)
    if not own:
        return False
    on_main = _diff_names(repo, base_ref, tip_sha)  # two-dot, per B8
    return own.isdisjoint(on_main)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Candidate-PR set (bulk, never per-branch - see the plan's "Candidate-PR
# set for B" section)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _extract_merge_commit_oid(pr: Dict) -> Optional[str]:
    mc = pr.get("mergeCommit")
    if isinstance(mc, dict):
        return mc.get("oid")
    if isinstance(mc, str):
        return mc or None
    return None


# GraphQL document for the merged-PR window. `totalCount` is selected on
# EVERY page deliberately: it is the completeness proof (nodes retrieved
# must equal it), and re-reading it per page also makes a window that moved
# mid-run surface as a mismatch rather than as a silently short set.
#
# The node shape is byte-compatible with what
# `gh pr list --json number,headRefName,headRefOid,mergeCommit` returned,
# so `_candidates_for_branch` and `_extract_merge_commit_oid` consume it
# unchanged and a `--pr-data` file captured from EITHER source stays valid.
_MERGED_PR_QUERY = """\
query($owner:String!,$name:String!,$pageSize:Int!,$endCursor:String){
  repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){
    pullRequests(states:MERGED,first:$pageSize,after:$endCursor){
      totalCount
      pageInfo{ hasNextPage endCursor }
      nodes{ number headRefName headRefOid mergeCommit{ oid } }
    }
  }
}
"""


def _gh_repo_slug(repo: str) -> Optional[Tuple[str, str]]:
    """(owner, name) for --repo's checkout, or None when gh cannot say.

    `gh pr list` inferred the repository from its cwd; `gh api graphql`
    cannot, so the slug must be resolved explicitly. EVERY failure path
    here returns None, which the caller converts into exactly the same
    degraded outcome an absent or erroring `gh` produced before this
    change - a resolution failure can only remove evidence, never add it.
    """
    try:
        proc = _run(
            ["gh", "repo", "view", "--json", "nameWithOwner"],
            cwd=repo,
            timeout=GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS,
        )
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - any gh failure, TIMEOUT INCLUDED, degrades
        return None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        payload = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    except json.JSONDecodeError:
        return None
    slug = payload.get("nameWithOwner") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(slug, str) or "/" not in slug:
        return None
    owner, name = slug.split("/", 1)
    if not owner or not name:
        return None
    return owner, name


def _load_merged_prs(
    repo: str, pr_data_file: Optional[str], no_gh: bool
) -> Tuple[List[Dict], bool, Optional[str]]:
    """Returns (merged_prs, degraded, incomplete_reason).

    degraded          - True when NO PR data was fetched at all (gh absent,
                        unauthenticated, erroring, --no-gh, or the very
                        first page failing). L2/L3 cannot fire.
    incomplete_reason - None ONLY when the merged-PR window is PROVEN
                        complete, i.e. the number of nodes retrieved equals
                        the `pullRequests` connection's own `totalCount`.
                        Otherwise a short operator-facing phrase naming why
                        completeness could not be established. Any non-None
                        value is treated exactly as the old `truncated=True`
                        was: the window may be short, and absence of a
                        match is NEVER proof that a branch is unmerged.

    Fail-safe contract, both directions (unchanged by the switch to
    pagination):

      Too FEW rows (a dropped page, an early API error, a page-cap
      exhaustion, an unreconciled count) - a genuinely merged branch simply
      finds no candidate PR, so L2/L3 cannot fire and it falls through to
      SKIP_UNPROVEN. Strictly fewer deletions. Safe.

      Too MANY / WRONG rows (a duplicated page, a cursor replay, a
      fabricated node) - cannot manufacture a "this branch is merged"
      verdict on its own, because every layer re-checks the row against
      local objects before acting on it: L2 re-derives patch-id AND
      byte-level hunk content, and L3 requires the PR's mergeCommit to be
      reachable on the base ref and the branch tip to be a real ancestor of
      the recorded head. A PR row only ever nominates a CANDIDATE for those
      local checks to accept or reject.

      The precise limit of that, stated rather than overclaimed: L3 does
      trust the row's PAIRING of `headRefOid` with `mergeCommit`. It never
      verifies that the head is contained in the merge commit, so a row
      pairing a real head with an unrelated already-merged mergeCommit
      would pass L3. That is pre-existing behavior, unchanged here, and
      sound for rows that genuinely came from the API; it is recorded
      because "no layer trusts a PR row" would be a stronger claim than
      this code supports. Safe under the failure modes above, which
      duplicate or drop API-sourced rows rather than synthesize pairings.

    So a pagination bug degrades evidence in the first direction and is
    inert in the second - which is precisely the property the pre-existing
    500-row truncation NOTE asserted, preserved here in substance.
    """
    if pr_data_file:
        path = Path(pr_data_file)
        if not path.is_file():
            raise ValueError(f"--pr-data file not found: {pr_data_file}")
        try:
            data = json.loads(path.read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
        except json.JSONDecodeError as exc:
            raise ValueError(f"--pr-data file is not valid JSON: {pr_data_file}: {exc}") from exc
        if not isinstance(data, list):
            raise ValueError(f"--pr-data file must contain a JSON array: {pr_data_file}")
        # Injected data is taken at face value, exactly as before: the
        # caller asserted this set, so there is no connection totalCount to
        # reconcile it against and nothing to report as incomplete.
        return data, False, None

    if no_gh or shutil.which("gh") is None:
        return [], True, None

    slug = _gh_repo_slug(repo)
    if slug is None:
        return [], True, None
    owner, name = slug

    nodes: List[Dict] = []
    total_count: Optional[int] = None
    cursor: Optional[str] = None
    saw_last_page = False
    deadline = time.monotonic() + PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS

    for _page in range(PR_MAX_PAGES):
        if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
            return _incomplete_window(
                nodes,
                f"the {PR_FETCH_DEADLINE_SECONDS:.0f}s merged-PR fetch deadline elapsed "
                "with more pages pending",
            )
        cmd = [
            "gh",
            "api",
            "graphql",
            "-f",
            f"query={_MERGED_PR_QUERY}",
            # `-f` (raw string) rather than `-F` (typed) for owner and name:
            # `-F` infers a type from the literal, so an owner or repository
            # named e.g. `2048` would be sent as an Int and rejected by the
            # `$owner:String!` / `$name:String!` declarations. `pageSize`
            # genuinely IS an Int (`$pageSize:Int!`) and so genuinely needs
            # `-F`; `endCursor` below is `String` and takes `-f`.
            "-f",
            f"owner={owner}",
            "-f",
            f"name={name}",
            "-F",
            f"pageSize={PR_PAGE_SIZE}",
        ]
        # The first request omits $endCursor entirely rather than sending an
        # empty string: `after: ""` is not a valid cursor, whereas an
        # omitted nullable variable means exactly "start from the beginning".
        if cursor is not None:
            cmd += ["-f", f"endCursor={cursor}"]

        try:
            proc = _run(cmd, cwd=repo, timeout=GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            # Named separately from the generic failure below so the NOTE
            # tells the operator it was a HANG rather than an error, and so
            # a hung call can never be mistaken for a complete window.
            return _incomplete_window(
                nodes,
                f"a gh api graphql call exceeded the {GH_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:.0f}s timeout",
            )
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - any gh failure degrades, never raises
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql could not be invoked")
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql exited nonzero")
        try:
            payload = json.loads(proc.stdout)
        except json.JSONDecodeError:
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql returned unparseable JSON")

        conn = _pull_requests_connection(payload)
        if conn is None:
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql returned an unexpected shape")

        page_total = conn.get("totalCount")
        if isinstance(page_total, int) and not isinstance(page_total, bool):
            total_count = page_total

        page_nodes = conn.get("nodes")
        if not isinstance(page_nodes, list):
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql returned no nodes array")
        nodes.extend(n for n in page_nodes if isinstance(n, dict))

        page_info = conn.get("pageInfo")
        if not isinstance(page_info, dict):
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql returned no pageInfo")
        if not page_info.get("hasNextPage"):
            saw_last_page = True
            break
        next_cursor = page_info.get("endCursor")
        if not isinstance(next_cursor, str) or not next_cursor:
            return _incomplete_window(nodes, "gh api graphql returned no endCursor")
        cursor = next_cursor

    if not saw_last_page:
        return _incomplete_window(
            nodes,
            f"pagination hit the {PR_MAX_PAGES}-page safety bound with more pages pending",
        )

    # THE completeness proof. Equality between what was retrieved and what
    # the connection itself says exists is the only thing that licenses
    # reporting this window as complete; every other outcome is reported as
    # incomplete and degrades evidence exactly as truncation used to.
    if not isinstance(total_count, int) or isinstance(total_count, bool):
        return _incomplete_window(
            nodes, "the connection reported no totalCount to reconcile against"
        )
    if len(nodes) != total_count:
        return _incomplete_window(
            nodes, f"retrieved {len(nodes)} of {total_count} merged PRs reported by the API"
        )

    return nodes, False, None


def _incomplete_window(nodes: List[Dict], reason: str) -> Tuple[List[Dict], bool, str]:
    """Package a merged-PR window that could NOT be proven complete.

    An empty result is reported as `degraded` (no PR data at all was
    obtained - the same state an absent `gh` produces), a partial result as
    non-degraded but incomplete. Both carry the reason so the operator is
    told either way, and NEITHER can widen the delete set: the reason is
    only ever consumed to print a NOTE and never feeds any layer.
    """
    if not nodes:
        return [], True, reason
    return nodes, False, reason


def _pull_requests_connection(payload: object) -> Optional[Dict]:
    """data.repository.pullRequests from a gh api graphql response, or None.

    Returns None on any missing or mistyped hop AND whenever the response
    carries a GraphQL `errors` array, so a partial-data error response
    (HTTP 200 with `data` present alongside `errors`, which `gh` does not
    always surface as a nonzero exit) is classified as incomplete rather
    than silently consumed as a short but valid page.
    """
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return None
    if payload.get("errors"):
        return None
    data = payload.get("data")
    if not isinstance(data, dict):
        return None
    repository = data.get("repository")
    if not isinstance(repository, dict):
        return None
    conn = repository.get("pullRequests")
    return conn if isinstance(conn, dict) else None


def _candidates_for_branch(
    branch: str, tip_sha: str, upstream: Optional[str], base_ref: str, merged_prs: List[Dict]
) -> List[Dict]:
    upstream_stripped = None
    if upstream and upstream != base_ref:
        upstream_stripped = upstream.split("/", 1)[1] if "/" in upstream else upstream

    result = []
    for pr in merged_prs:
        mc_oid = _extract_merge_commit_oid(pr)
        if not mc_oid:
            continue
        head_oid = pr.get("headRefOid")
        head_name = pr.get("headRefName")
        matches = (
            head_oid == tip_sha
            or head_name == branch
            or (upstream_stripped is not None and head_name == upstream_stripped)
        )
        if matches:
            result.append({"number": pr.get("number"), "headRefName": head_name, "headRefOid": head_oid, "mergeCommit": mc_oid})
    return result


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-branch evidence resolution
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def evaluate_branch(
    repo: str,
    branch: str,
    base_ref: str,
    base_branches: Set[str],
    checked_out: Set[str],
    merged_prs: List[Dict],
    degraded: bool,
    incomplete_reason: Optional[str],
) -> Dict:
    """Resolves one local branch against the guards and layers, returning a
    dict with at least a `branch` and `outcome` key. `outcome` is one of
    SKIP_BASE_BRANCH, SKIP_CHECKED_OUT, SKIP_NO_MERGE_BASE, DELETE,
    SKIP_UNPROVEN. A DELETE result additionally carries `layer` (which of
    L1-L4 fired first) and `tip_sha`.

    `degraded` and `incomplete_reason` are BOTH required (no defaults) and
    both describe the same thing from the caller's side: whether the
    merged-PR window this function is handed can be relied on to answer
    "does a merged PR exist for this branch?" at all. `degraded` means no
    window was obtained; a non-None `incomplete_reason` means one was, but
    its completeness could not be proven. Either state makes an absent
    candidate UNINFORMATIVE, so `pr_state` becomes `"not_checked"` rather
    than the affirmative `"NONE"` - see the `pr_state` assignment below.
    """
    if branch in base_branches:
        return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "SKIP_BASE_BRANCH"}
    if branch in checked_out:
        return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "SKIP_CHECKED_OUT"}

    tip_sha = _rev_parse(repo, branch)
    if tip_sha is None:
        return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "SKIP_NO_MERGE_BASE"}

    base_sha = _merge_base(repo, branch, base_ref)
    if base_sha is None:
        return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "SKIP_NO_MERGE_BASE"}

    layer: Optional[str] = None
    merge_evidence = "unmerged"
    if _is_ancestor(repo, branch, base_ref):
        merge_evidence = "merged"
        layer = "L1"

    upstream = _upstream(repo, branch)
    candidates = _candidates_for_branch(branch, tip_sha, upstream, base_ref, merged_prs)
    if candidates:
        pr_state = "MERGED"
    else:
        # "NONE" is an AFFIRMATIVE claim - no merged PR exists for this
        # branch - and may only be made from a window that was both
        # obtained AND proven complete. `DispositionFacts`' own docstring
        # (bin/tests/worktree_model.py) states the contract this honors: a
        # caller that cannot determine a fact must pass "not_checked", which
        # every gate treats as inconclusive-and-fails-closed. Asserting
        # "NONE" off an incomplete window would be reporting a fact the tool
        # has just finished proving it could not enumerate. It is NOT
        # sufficient that the shared model happens to collapse the two
        # values today: `_check_pr_state_strict` treats them alike and
        # `MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER` omits `origin_reachable`, so this is inert
        # at present - but that is a coincidence of the current gates, not a
        # property this call site may rely on.
        pr_state = "not_checked" if (degraded or incomplete_reason) else "NONE"

    if layer is None:
        for cand in candidates:
            mc = cand["mergeCommit"]
            if not _commit_exists(repo, mc):
                continue
            if not _is_ancestor(repo, mc, base_ref):
                continue
            if _l2_match(repo, base_sha, tip_sha, mc):
                layer = "L2"
                break

    if layer is None:
        for cand in candidates:
            mc = cand["mergeCommit"]
            head_oid = cand["headRefOid"]
            if not head_oid:
                continue
            if not _is_ancestor(repo, mc, base_ref):
                continue
            if not _commit_exists(repo, head_oid):
                continue
            if _is_ancestor(repo, tip_sha, head_oid):
                layer = "L3"
                break

    if layer is None and _l4_match(repo, base_sha, tip_sha, base_ref):
        layer = "L4"

    content_subsumed = layer is not None and layer != "L1"

    facts = DispositionFacts(
        dirty_status="not_checked",
        head_reachable="not_checked",
        ls_remote_status="not_checked",
        merge_evidence=merge_evidence,
        content_subsumption="subsumed" if content_subsumed else "not_subsumed",
        pr_state=pr_state,
        # DS-196: this tool computes no origin-reachability evidence, and
        # disposition_for_orphan_branch never consults this field (see
        # worktree_model.py's _check_origin_reachable / MERGE_EVIDENCE_ORDER
        # docstrings) - the constructor still requires it explicitly, since
        # DispositionFacts carries no field defaults.
        origin_reachable="not_checked",
    )
    disposition = disposition_for_orphan_branch(branch, facts, base_branches=tuple(base_branches))

    if disposition is Disposition.ELIGIBLE:
        return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "DELETE", "layer": layer or "L1", "tip_sha": tip_sha}

    # SKIP_PR_MERGED_UNPROVEN and the model's final-fallback
    # SKIP_AMBIGUOUS_NO_PR both display as SKIP_UNPROVEN here, matching the
    # plan's own outcome vocabulary - see the module docstring.
    return {"branch": branch, "outcome": "SKIP_UNPROVEN", "tip_sha": tip_sha}


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ledger (Amendment B3)
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _append_ledger(repo: str, line: str) -> None:
    """Append ONE `<branch> <tip-sha>\\n` line to the ledger, true append
    mode (Skeptic round-2 M1/m4 fix). Two changes from the original
    design:

    1. Called ONCE PER BRANCH, immediately BEFORE that branch's own
       `git branch -D` (write-ahead), never batched after the fact - a
       crash or ledger-write failure partway through a run can now only
       ever cost the branches not yet reached, not every branch already
       processed.
    2. True `open(..., "a")` append, not `_lib.atomic_write`'s
       read-then-write-whole-file pattern. `atomic_write` is safe for a
       single writer but is a last-write-wins race for two CONCURRENT
       writers targeting the same path (its own docstring says so) - two
       overlapping session-start prunes could each read the same "prior"
       content and one process's write would silently discard the other's
       entries. POSIX `O_APPEND` (which `"a"` mode maps to) is what
       actually avoids that race for a shared append-only log.
    """
    ledger_path = Path(repo) / LEDGER_RELATIVE_PATH
    ledger_path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    with open(ledger_path, "a", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        fh.write(line)


# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_args(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> argparse.Namespace:
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="ds-branch-prune",
        description="Squash-aware local branch prune via a four-layer subsumption predicate (DS-153).",
    )
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=".")
    # Default None, NOT "origin/main": a hardcoded base evaluates every
    # branch against the wrong ref on any repo whose integration branch is
    # not `main`, proving nothing. On None, main() resolves it through the
    # shared `resolve_base_branch` (bin/_lib.py) - the same resolver
    # bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees uses. An explicit --base is still taken
    # verbatim with no validation.
    p.add_argument("--base", default=None)
    p.add_argument("--explain", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--pr-data")
    p.add_argument("--no-gh", action="store_true")
    p.add_argument("--nudge-threshold", type=int, default=20)
    return p.parse_args(argv)


def main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:
    args = parse_args(argv)

    try:
        repo = _resolve_repo(args.repo)
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # `--pr-data` validation runs BEFORE base resolution, deliberately. An
    # unusable --pr-data file is a USAGE error (exit 1) and must stay one
    # even on a repo whose base cannot be resolved - the base fail-safe
    # below exits 0, so resolving first would silently downgrade a usage
    # error to a clean skip. This is the only exit-1 path inside
    # `_load_merged_prs`, and (with --pr-data set) it touches no network.
    try:
        merged_prs, degraded, incomplete_reason = _load_merged_prs(
            repo, args.pr_data, args.no_gh
        )
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - internal error, report and exit 1
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: internal error: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    # Base-branch resolution. An explicit --base is returned verbatim and
    # unvalidated (tier "explicit"); otherwise the shared resolver in
    # bin/_lib.py runs its documented tier order - AGENTS.md `BASE_BRANCH:`
    # (authoritative), origin/HEAD, local develop, local development, then
    # main falling back to master.
    resolved_base, base_source, base_diagnostics = resolve_base_branch(
        repo, args.base, prog="ds-branch-prune"
    )
    for line in base_diagnostics:
        print(line, file=sys.stderr)
    if resolved_base is None:
        # FAIL SAFE, and specifically fail safe on the DESTRUCTIVE side:
        # this tool deletes branches, so an unresolvable base must delete
        # nothing rather than fall back to a guessed ref. Every candidate
        # tried is already named in base_diagnostics above. Exit 0, not 1 -
        # exit 1 stays reserved for a genuine internal/usage error, and
        # this runs unconditionally at session start where a nonzero exit
        # would read as a broken preflight (same contract as
        # bin/ds-cleanup-worktrees' own unresolvable-base path).
        print(
            "ds-branch-prune: base could not be resolved - branch prune skipped this run "
            "(no branches deleted, no ledger entry written).",
            file=sys.stderr,
        )
        print(
            "ds-branch-prune: base=unresolved "
            f"mode={_compose_mode(degraded, args.dry_run, skipped=True)} "
            "branches=0 deletions=0 skips=0"
        )
        return 0
    if base_source != "explicit":
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: base auto-resolved to '{resolved_base}' via {base_source}")

    base_local = (
        resolved_base.split("/", 1)[1] if resolved_base.startswith("origin/") else resolved_base
    )
    # M3 fix (Skeptic round 2), as amended by the worktree-reap calibration
    # work: G0 in the plan's normative predicate protects "B == resolved
    # BASE_BRANCH". The session-start call site still passes no --base, but
    # BASE_BRANCH is no longer unresolved at that point - this script now
    # resolves it itself, above, so `base_local` is the ACTUAL resolved base
    # rather than a hardcoded `main`. `develop`/`development` stay in the
    # guard set UNCONDITIONALLY regardless (not merely when one of them is
    # the resolved base): a develop-based workflow's integration branch must
    # survive even a run whose resolution landed elsewhere.
    base_branches = set(DEFAULT_BASE_BRANCHES) | {"develop", "development", base_local}

    try:
        checked_out = _checked_out_branches(repo)
        branches = _list_local_branches(repo)

        results = [
            evaluate_branch(
                repo,
                b,
                resolved_base,
                base_branches,
                checked_out,
                merged_prs,
                degraded,
                incomplete_reason,
            )
            for b in sorted(branches)
        ]
    except ValueError as exc:
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - internal error, report and exit 1
        print(f"ds-branch-prune: internal error: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1

    delete_results = [r for r in results if r["outcome"] == "DELETE"]
    skip_counts = Counter(r["outcome"] for r in results if r["outcome"] != "DELETE")

    # `degraded` (gh unavailable) and `args.dry_run` are independent axes - a
    # run can be both at once, and collapsing them into a single either/or
    # chain would mask whichever fact loses the tiebreak. Both must remain
    # visible when both hold, and a --dry-run invocation must never report
    # the bare "live" an operator would read as "deletions already ran".
    # `_compose_mode` is shared with the unresolvable-base line above so
    # neither can drop an axis the other consults.
    mode = _compose_mode(degraded, args.dry_run)
    print(
        f"ds-branch-prune: base={resolved_base} mode={mode} branches={len(results)} "
        f"deletions={len(delete_results)} skips={len(results) - len(delete_results)}"
    )
    for outcome in ("SKIP_BASE_BRANCH", "SKIP_CHECKED_OUT", "SKIP_NO_MERGE_BASE", "SKIP_UNPROVEN"):
        if skip_counts.get(outcome):
            print(f"  {outcome}: {skip_counts[outcome]}")

    if incomplete_reason:
        # Preserved from the old 500-row truncation NOTE: the operator is
        # still told whenever the merged-PR window is not provably
        # complete, and the guarantee that absence of a match is never
        # read as proof of unmerged is restated verbatim.
        print(
            f"NOTE: the merged-PR window could not be proven complete ({incomplete_reason}) "
            "- L2/L3 evidence may be short; absence of a match is never treated as proof "
            "a branch is unmerged."
        )
    if degraded:
        print(
            "NOTE: degraded mode (gh unavailable/erroring/--no-gh) - only ancestry (L1) and "
            "content-on-main (L4) evidence available; this can only delete FEWER branches than "
            "a full run, never more."
        )
    unproven = skip_counts.get("SKIP_UNPROVEN", 0)
    if unproven >= args.nudge_threshold:
        print(
            f"NOTE: {unproven} branches are accumulating as SKIP_UNPROVEN "
            f"(>= nudge threshold {args.nudge_threshold}) - consider a manual review pass."
        )

    if args.explain:
        print("-- per-branch --")
        for r in results:
            if r["outcome"] == "DELETE":
                print(f"{r['branch']}: DELETE via {r['layer']}")
            else:
                print(f"{r['branch']}: {r['outcome']}")

    if not args.dry_run and delete_results:
        for r in delete_results:
            # M1 fix (Skeptic round 2): the ledger entry is written BEFORE
            # the destructive `git branch -D`, one branch at a time, never
            # batched after every delete. `git branch -D` destroys the
            # branch's own reflog outright, so the ledger is the SOLE
            # durable recovery record - it must exist before the act it
            # documents, not after. A ledger-write failure is therefore a
            # HARD STOP on any further deletion this run (not a crash): the
            # branches already deleted above remain deleted with their
            # entries safely recorded; nothing after this point in
            # `delete_results` is touched.
            ledger_line = f"{r['branch']} {r['tip_sha']}\n"
            try:
                _append_ledger(repo, ledger_line)
            except OSError as exc:
                print(
                    f"ds-branch-prune: ERROR: failed to write the deletion ledger entry "
                    f"for {r['branch']} before deleting it - halting further deletions "
                    f"this run ({exc}). git branch -D was NOT attempted for {r['branch']} "
                    "or any branch after it in this run's ordering.",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )
                break

            proc = _run(["git", "-C", repo, "branch", "-D", r["branch"]])
            if proc.returncode == 0:
                print(f"{r['branch']} {r['tip_sha']}")
            else:
                # Amendment B11: report and continue, exit 0.
                print(
                    f"WARNING: failed to delete {r['branch']} (git branch -D exit "
                    f"{proc.returncode}): {proc.stderr.strip()}",
                    file=sys.stderr,
                )

    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
